  "question": {
    "text": "If the length of a wire is made double and radius is halved of its respective values. Then, the Young&#39;s modulus of the material of the wire will :"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Remain same",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "Become <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn></math> times its initial value",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Become <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msup><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>th</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></mrow></msup><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math> of its initial value",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Become <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math> times its initial value",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Young&#39;s modulus is the ratio of stress to strain.</p><p>Young&#39;s modulus is a property of a material, and it does not depend on the geometry i.e. length and the radius of the wire. Therefore, the Young&#39;s modulus of the wire will remain same.</p></blockquote>"
  }
